        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Existing project?</strong> Claude Code and Devin CLI: <code>/soleur:sync</code>. Grok Build and OpenCode: <code>/sync</code>. Codex: <code>$soleur:sync</code>. Analyze the codebase and populate the knowledge base.<br>
          <strong>Starting fresh?</strong> Claude Code and Devin CLI: <code>/soleur:go</code>. Grok Build and OpenCode: <code>/go</code>. Codex: <code>$soleur:go</code>. Describe what you need.
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Updating later?</strong> It takes two commands, and the first one looks like enough on its own. <code>claude plugin marketplace update soleur-marketplace</code> advances the marketplace checkout; <code>claude plugin update soleur@soleur-marketplace</code> updates the plugin you actually run. Name the marketplace in both halves &mdash; on current releases the bare plugin name can fail with <code>Plugin not found</code>, and if you added this repository directly the id is <code>soleur</code>, so run <code>claude plugin list</code> and use whatever it prints beside Soleur. Skipping the second command leaves you on the old version while the new one looks installed &mdash; and if a fix still isn&rsquo;t taking effect, <code>claude plugin uninstall soleur@soleur-marketplace &amp;&amp; claude plugin install soleur@soleur-marketplace</code> is the step guaranteed to converge them.
        </div>

        <h3 class="section-subtitle" style="margin-top: var(--space-8);">The Workflow</h3>
        <p>Soleur follows a structured 6-step workflow for software development:</p>

        <p>The 6-step workflow (each harness invokes it with its own entry command &mdash; see the table above):</p>

        <div class="commands-list">
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>1. brainstorm</code>
            <p>Explore what to build</p>
          </div>
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>2. plan</code>
            <p>Create a structured implementation plan</p>
          </div>
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>3. work</code>
            <p>Execute the plan with quality checks</p>
          </div>
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>4. review</code>
            <p>Run multi-agent code review</p>
          </div>
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>5. compound</code>
            <p>Document learnings for future reference</p>
          </div>
          <div class="command-item">
            <code>6. ship</code>
            <p>Prepare the feature for production deployment</p>
          </div>
        </div>

