    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <section class="landing-section">
      <div class="landing-section-inner">
        <p class="section-label">Common Questions</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">What do I need to run Soleur?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Nothing to install. The hosted version runs in your browser once your access opens -- bring a workspace and your team. Prefer to self-host? The self-hosted version runs inside your own agentic harness -- Claude Code, Grok Build, Codex, Devin CLI, or OpenCode. Setup is two commands on Claude Code; the other harnesses install from this repository and each has its own setup guide.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">Why a waitlist?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">We're onboarding a founding cohort of 10 teams by hand so every workflow lands right. Access opens in waves as the hosted infrastructure matures -- we ship weekly and move people off the list as capacity grows. Want to skip the line? Run the self-hosted version today.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">Does Soleur work on Windows, Linux, and macOS?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">The self-hosted version runs anywhere its harnesses run: Claude Code, Grok Build, Codex, and Devin CLI all support Linux, macOS, and Windows via WSL, and OpenCode follows its own platform support &mdash; install inside whichever one you already use. The hosted version will be accessible from any browser on any operating system.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">How much does Soleur cost?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">See the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> for hosted plans (coming soon; join the waitlist) -- founder pricing starts at $49/month. The self-hosted version is free; your costs depend entirely on the model provider you use through your harness.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">What is the difference between /soleur:go and individual skills?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer"><code>/soleur:go</code> (Claude Code and Devin CLI), <code>/go</code> (Grok Build and OpenCode), or <code>$soleur:go</code> (Codex) is the unified entry point that classifies your intent and routes to the right workflow automatically. Individual skills like brainstorm, plan, work, and review can be invoked directly when you know exactly which stage you need &mdash; Skill tool <code>soleur:&lt;skill&gt;</code> on Claude Code; Read that skill&rsquo;s <code>SKILL.md</code> in this process on Grok Build; the skill tool with the bare id on OpenCode.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">Can I use Soleur with an existing project?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Yes. Claude Code and Devin CLI: <code>/soleur:sync</code>. Grok Build and OpenCode: <code>/sync</code>. Codex: <code>$soleur:sync</code>. Analyze the codebase and populate the knowledge base with your project's conventions, architecture, and patterns. Soleur adapts to your existing codebase rather than requiring a fresh start.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">What is the difference between the hosted version and self-hosted?</summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Both versions ship the same agents and skills. The self-hosted version runs inside your own harness &mdash; Claude Code, Grok Build, Codex, Devin CLI, or OpenCode &mdash; with full access to your own files. The hosted version (coming soon) adds a web dashboard, managed infrastructure, real-time agent orchestration, team collaboration, and email support. Both access the same {{ stats.agents }} agents and {{ stats.skills }} skills.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

